yle, with static SigV4 credentials, plain HTTP (TLS is terminated in front of the gateway), and retries and stalled-stream protection off. Its error is typed: HTTP status, `<Code>`, `<Message>`, and the `x-amz-request-id` read from that header itself. Connect, operation, body-idle, and whole-body deadlines bound every wait (the last so a peer that trickles a GET body inside every idle window still cannot hold a worker past it), and object bodies stream both ways, so what the client holds of an object does not grow with its size. A PUT reports a receipt only if the caller's source had ended, that is, had given its whole declared length and then reported its end, by the time the response head was read off the socket; a success that arrives sooner is the typed body error `AcknowledgedEarly`, carrying that response's request id, and from that moment the source is not polled again (#854). The client cannot see what the server read, nor an answer its own kernel has received but the HTTP stack has not yet read, so a receipt says only what the client handed over before it read the answer. No PUT outlives its call: each runs on a runtime and a thread of its own, over a connection that is never pooled, and that runtime is shut down, dropping the source and closing the connection, before the outcome is returned (or promptly after, if the caller drops the call). The price is one thread and one fresh TCP connection per PUT, which the client closes, so each PUT leaves a client socket in `TIME_WAIT`. That caps sustained PUTs between one client address and one gateway address at about the number of ephemeral ports divided by the `TIME_WAIT` time, about 470 a second with Linux's defaults (28,232 ports, 60 s); past that, `connect` fails and the client reports `NoResponse`, which the failure taxonomy (proposal 0017 §7) would count against the server as `availability`. The cap is a host default, not a hard ceiling: it holds per pair of client address and gateway address, shared by every worker on that client, and an operator raises it with more client or gateway addresses, a wider `net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range`, or `net.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse = 1` (Linux's default, `2`, reuses ports on loopback only). The AWS SDK is the only shipped dependency `validate` adds; Wyrd crates appear only as dev-dependencies, for the in-process gateway its tests run against. The binary still echoes its resolved configuration (the access-key id, never the secret) and exits without issuing a request; scenarios arrive with #743.
